A||||4||88formula||I've got stock camshaft specs for 93 Camaro 3.4 ||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||07-22-2000||07:34 PM||||I searched everywhere and asked everyone I knew about the camshaft specifications for the stock 93 Camaro engine I am installing into my 88 formula and no one had the answer. I decided to get them myself and got a degree wheel to find out what they were. I wanted to run the numbers on Desktop Dyno to see if this camshaft that came with the Camaro engine would make the kind of power I am looking for. I was hoping that it would be the same cam as GM put in there crate motor but this was not the case. The camshaft that is in the 93 Camaro 3.4 engine is a little milder than the one that comes with the crate motor. The GM performance crate engine has 204 degrees intake and 216 degrees exhaust of duration measured @ .050”. The Camaro engine has 195 degrees intake and 214 degrees exhaust of duration measured @ .050”. The crate engine has a 107 degree lobe separation angle and the Camaro engine has 105.8 degrees. The Crate engine has .427 intake/ .454 exhaust of valve lift and the Camaro engine has .390 intake/ .405 exhaust of valve lift. The crate engine makes slightly more mid to upper range horsepower and torque than the Camaro engine but there is not a big difference between the two too my surprise. Both engines have a compression ratio of 9.0 to 1. <P>I also have the head flow bench numbers for the ported and stock heads of the fiero 2.8 for the Desktop Dyno program that I got from someone who had then tested on a flow bench.<P>Here is the camshaft specifications for the stock  93 Camaro 3.4  engine.<P>Duration @ .050” intake/exhaust 195/214<P>Lobe separation angle 108.5<P>Lift intake/exhaust .390/.405<P>Intake centerline 110<P>Valve overlap –7<P>Compression ratio 9.0<P>IVO (BTDC) –13<BR>IVC (ABDC)  28<BR>EVO (BBDC)  28<BR>EVC (ATDC)  6<BR> <BR>Horsepower 166 @ 5000<BR>Torque         208 @ 3000 with no modifications to engine.<P>The crate engine.<P>Duration @ .050” intake/exhaust 204/216<P>Lobe separation angle 107<P>Lift intake/exhaust .427/.454<P>Intake centerline 105<P>Valve overlap –4<P>Compression ratio 9.0<P>IVO (BTDC) –3<BR>IVC (ABDC)  27<BR>EVO (BBDC)  37<BR>EVC (ATDC)  -1<BR> <BR>Horsepower 169 @ 5000<BR>Torque         210 @ 3000 with no modifications to crate engine.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula4ever||07-22-2000||10:06 PM||||88Formula thanks so much. I've been wondering about that myself. I'm putting a 95 3.4 into my Formula and was thinking of putting a new, bigger cam in, but I think I'll keep it stock. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||07-23-2000||09:51 PM||||I'm glade I could help you. <P>I will be getting the new Dyno drag strip to test which cam would give the best times down the quarter mile. You can take the dyno simulations from the Desktop dyno program and download them into Dyno drag strip to see what the cam will give you for acceleration and what the best shift points would be. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||07-24-2000||08:59 PM||||88- I used the specs for the stock cam on the desktop dyno, but added "performance exhaust" and the power went up to 175. I used this simulation in the desktop dragstrip with the 4.10 four speed and after playing with launch and shift RPM's for a while I came up with this: launch at 1200 (to keep from smoking the tires)and shift at 6,000 for each change and it ran a "best" of 14.47 in the simulated quarter mile. I doubt the car would really be that fast, but it is a nice thought.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||07-24-2000||10:01 PM||||Cooter I put in the head flow numbers in the program for a set of ported heads and got 174 HP. I also have the flow numbers with stock heads and I got 166 HP with the same combination. The flow numbers preset in the program are higher than what the fiero heads actually flow. Did you try the drag that with the Crate engine camshaft?<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||1
